WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.900
In a room hot enough to cook an egg, why didn't the man cook?

2
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:14.120
In 1775, Charles Blagden sat in a room heated close to the boiling point of water.

3
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.100
On another visit, eggs in that room roasted hard.

4
00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:20.353
Start with the egg.

5
00:00:21.693 --> 00:00:23.353
Hot air pours heat into it.

6
00:00:24.273 --> 00:00:25.473
Nothing pushes back.

7
00:00:26.653 --> 00:00:28.993
So the egg keeps warming toward the air's temperature.

8
00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:31.533
It cooks along the way.

9
00:00:32.533 --> 00:00:36.653
Now the man. The same air pours heat into him.

10
00:00:37.813 --> 00:00:39.013
Call that flow the leak.

11
00:00:40.213 --> 00:00:42.813
Yet the thermometer under his tongue barely moved.

12
00:00:44.073 --> 00:00:46.313
It stayed about one degree above his usual.

13
00:00:47.417 --> 00:00:50.037
Something in his body pulled the other way.

14
00:00:51.077 --> 00:00:52.077
Call it push-back.

15
00:00:53.397 --> 00:00:55.337
Leak drags the inside toward the outside.

16
00:00:56.737 --> 00:00:58.657
Push-back drags it toward a fixed value.

17
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.120
Push-back comes from a loop with five parts.

18
00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.480
First, the regulated variable, the quantity being held.

19
00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.300
Here it is core body temperature.

20
00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:13.940
Second, the set point, the reference value it is held around.

21
00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.080
Third, the sensor, which measures the variable.

22
00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:22.980
Temperature-sensitive nerve cells in the skin and brain do this.

23
00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:28.160
Fourth, the integrator, a region of the brain called the hypothalamus.

24
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:33.120
It compares the sensed value with the set point and sends out a command.

25
00:01:34.283 --> 00:01:37.143
Fifth, the effectors, which change the variable.

26
00:01:38.363 --> 00:01:42.543
As he warms, skin blood vessels widen and carry heat to the surface.

27
00:01:44.143 --> 00:01:47.743
Sweat glands release sweat, which removes heat as it evaporates.

28
00:01:48.717 --> 00:01:51.857
Cool him instead, and other effectors take over.

29
00:01:53.117 --> 00:01:56.217
Skin vessels narrow to keep heat in, and muscles shiver.

30
00:01:57.537 --> 00:02:00.117
Either way, the push points back toward the set point.

31
00:02:01.277 --> 00:02:02.677
That is negative feedback.

32
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:06.540
So how far does the inside follow the outside?

33
00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.960
That depends on how strong push-back is compared with leak.

34
00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.480
Strength means the pull for each degree of gap.

35
00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:19.800
Set the two dials equal, so push-back is exactly as strong as leak.

36
00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.980
Then raise the outside by ten degrees.

37
00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.840
Does the inside follow all of it, half, or none?

38
00:02:33.333 --> 00:02:39.633
Half. In this simple model, the two pulls always balance once the inside settles.

39
00:02:40.973 --> 00:02:44.513
With equal strengths, they balance when the two gaps are equal, halfway.

40
00:02:45.733 --> 00:02:50.773
Turn push-back strength down to zero, and the inside follows the outside completely,

41
00:02:51.273 --> 00:02:54.993
like the egg. That end of the dial is a conformer.

42
00:02:56.373 --> 00:03:00.753
Turn push-back strength far above leak strength, and the inside barely moves.

43
00:03:01.973 --> 00:03:03.493
That end is a regulator.

44
00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:06.077
Back to the hot room.

45
00:03:07.457 --> 00:03:10.597
Blagden's body pushed back, so his temperature barely moved.

46
00:03:11.817 --> 00:03:15.297
The trials lasted minutes, so they show push-back, not its strength.

47
00:03:16.537 --> 00:03:18.257
The egg had no push-back at all.
